%!testlib
% What restore costs when a file was opened above the level it rewinds
% to, run through `xpost -q --no-sandbox -d null` by run-ps-test.sh.
%
% restore closes a file created since the corresponding save (PLRM
% 3.8.2), and it decides which files those are by looking for each of
% them on the stacks. How it looks is the whole subject here: a stack is a chain of
% thousand-object segments, and an index into one is reached by walking
% that chain from its root. Asking the stack for index 0, then index 1,
% and so on to the top walks the chain again for every element, which
% costs the stack's length once per element -- the length squared. One
% pass over the segments costs the length.
%
% Both ways look at every element and both reach the same answer, so
% nothing about which files are closed can tell them apart, and no short
% run can either: the two are the same on a stack of one segment and
% they differ by the depth on a deep one. What can tell them apart is
% how much of the chain was walked, so the interpreter counts the
% segments it steps over and the assertion is that count.
%
% A count and not a clock, for the reason the rest of the cost tests in
% this directory give: a bound stated in segments survives a loaded
% machine and a bound stated in seconds does not. It is also exact --
% the same program answers the same number every time -- which a ratio
% between two depths needs, because a ratio divides two readings and
% doubles whatever noise either of them carries.

/.vmstackwalk 1183615869 internaldict /.vmstackwalk get def

/SMALL 8000 def
/LARGE 16000 def

% Rewind past an open file with n objects under it, and answer the
% segments the sweep stepped over doing it.
%
% The file is named by nothing when the restore comes, which is the arm
% that makes the sweep read every stack to the end: a file the sweep
% does not find on any of them is one it closes, and it reaches that
% verdict only after looking everywhere. The other arm -- a file the
% sweep does find -- cannot be reached from a program at all, because a
% file made under the save and still named from a stack is what
% invalidrestore refuses (PLRM 3.7.3); its walk is this one's, stopped
% early, so this is the arm that bounds the cost.
%
% The file's entity is reclaimed before the next call, so that each
% depth is measured against the same number of files and the ratio below
% is a ratio of depths and nothing else. Without that the second arm
% would sweep for the first arm's file as well as its own.
/measure { % int  .  int
    /n exch def
    0 1 n 1 sub { pop 42 } for
    /sv save def
    (abcdefgh) 0 () /SubFileDecode filter pop
    .vmstackwalk pop
    sv restore
    .vmstackwalk pop
    exch sub /steps exch def
    clear
    2 vmreclaim
    steps
} def

% The name comes after the call: `measure` empties the operand stack on
% its way out, and a literal pushed before it would go with the rest.
SMALL measure /small exch def
LARGE measure /large exch def

% A hook stuck at one number would satisfy every bound below by saying
% nothing, so require first that it moved.
small 0 gt
    (the interpreter reports the stack segments it stepped over) assert

% The ceiling: a scan that walks each chain once steps over the segments
% the stacks hold, and a stack holds one segment per segment's worth of
% elements -- so the depth is a bound it cannot approach, let alone
% exceed. The point of stating the structural bound rather than the
% observed figure is that a scan walking the chain per index lands above
% this by the depth divided by the segment size, which is a factor that
% grows with the stack rather than a constant to be tuned.
small SMALL le
    (sweeping a deep stack stays within the bound) assert

large LARGE le
    (and stays within it over twice the depth) assert

% The property itself: twice the depth, and no more than twice the walk
% between them. Two and a half is the headroom, and a scan that re-walks
% the chain misses it by four rather than by a fraction.
large small 5 mul 2 idiv le
    (the cost of sweeping the stacks does not follow their depth squared) assert

checkcount 4 eq (every check was reached) assert

verdict
